gym: does a program leave a terminal the same whether it runs directly or
inside tmux, on terminals that behave differently from each other?

Two questions, two tools:

1. Where is tmux's own terminal emulation the odd one out?

     python3 gym/consensus.py [--fuzz N] [--only NAME] [--no-shrink]

   A program inside tmux is interpreted by tmux's emulator; the terminal only
   shows tmux's redraw. consensus.py runs each stream through a tmux pane and
   three independent engines - Ghostty (libghostty-vt), libvterm (Neovim,
   Vim) and Alacritty (alacritty_terminal) - and reports streams where the
   three agree and tmux alone differs, shrunk to the smallest stream that
   still shows it (shrink.py). Where the engines split among themselves it is
   a difference between terminals, not a tmux bug.

2. Does going through tmux change what a given terminal shows?

   vt.py is a terminal model whose behaviours real terminals disagree on are
   switches (Quirks), each classed as unstandardised or a deviation from DEC
   in the comment above the class. Profiles: XTERM, ITERM, PROMPT (iOS),
   GHOSTTY, TMUXPANE. It exists for what a real engine cannot give: terminals
   that cannot run here (Prompt on iOS) and events at an exact byte - a phone
   keyboard growing and shrinking the terminal without reporting it
   (Term.hidden_shift). validate.py checks the model against a tmux pane and
   (--ghostty) against Ghostty, stream by stream; --bisect and shrink.py find
   where they part.

Files
  vt.py          terminal model
  validate.py    model against a tmux pane or Ghostty
  consensus.py   tmux against Ghostty, libvterm and Alacritty
  shrink.py      delta debugging over escape sequences and characters
  probe.py       one small stream on the model and on Ghostty
  fuzz.py        seeded random streams
  ghostty/gvt.c, refs/lvt.c, refs/avt/   engine drivers; refs/build.sh builds
                 them (libghostty-vt needs Zig 0.16, libvterm-dev, cargo)

Streams are regress/render-parity.sh's cases (from its own cases.awk) plus
fuzz.py's. Every driver takes events at byte offsets: "OFFSET resize COLS
ROWS" and "OFFSET hidden K".
